&lt;div id="readme" class="md"&gt;&lt;div class="markdown-heading"&gt;
&lt;h1 class="heading-element"&gt;💼 FreelanceFlow AI — Private AI Expense &amp;amp; Invoice Copilot&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Built for the &lt;strong&gt;Hacktoberfest 2026 Week 2 Side Hustle challenge&lt;/strong&gt; (#devchallenge, #hf26challenge).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;FreelanceFlow AI&lt;/strong&gt; is a 100% local, privacy-first invoice and expense manager designed specifically for freelancers, solopreneurs, and contractors. It turns messy PDF invoices and receipt images into structured, categorized tax-ready expense records using a locally running &lt;strong&gt;Google Gemma&lt;/strong&gt; model served through &lt;strong&gt;Ollama&lt;/strong&gt; and a local &lt;strong&gt;SQLite&lt;/strong&gt; database.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="markdown-heading"&gt;
&lt;h2 class="heading-element"&gt;🌟 Why FreelanceFlow AI?&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As a freelancer, keeping track of deductible business expenses usually means juggling PDF receipts, taking screenshots of SaaS invoices, and spending hours each quarter manually typing numbers into spreadsheets.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Cloud AI alternatives exist, but they have major drawbacks:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Financial Privacy Risks:&lt;/strong&gt; Uploading bank statements, NDA-bound invoices, and personal receipts to third-party cloud LLM APIs poses severe confidentiality issues.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Recurring Token Costs:&lt;/strong&gt; Monthly SaaS fees and API bills eat away at freelance margins.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Vendor Lock-In:&lt;/strong&gt; Your expense history…&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="gh-btn-container"&gt;&lt;a class="gh-btn" href="https://github.com/snehalghadge59-blip/freelanceflow-ai" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;View on GitHub&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;p&gt;The repository includes the full source code, test suite, and Windows setup instructions:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;app.py&lt;/code&gt;: Modern SaaS UI, interactive charts, and review forms&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;database.py&lt;/code&gt;: Parameterized SQLite queries, duplicate hash checks, and demo data generator&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;ai_service.py&lt;/code&gt;: Local Ollama REST client, Gemma prompt engineer, and Pydantic validation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;invoice_parser.py&lt;/code&gt;: PyMuPDF native PDF text extraction, Tesseract OCR wrapper, and SHA-256 hashing&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;tests/&lt;/code&gt;: 13 passing unit tests verifying ACID compliance, Pydantic normalization, and document parsing&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


How I Built It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;FreelanceFlow AI is built entirely on open-source technologies and local-first architecture:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before a decision that matters — or whenever an AI sounds a little too sure of itself — it pays to ask more than one model. ChatGPT, Claude and Gemini have different strengths and different blind spots, and putting their answers side by side is the fastest way to catch what one of them missed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here are three ways to do it, and when each one makes sense.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Questions worth asking more than one AI
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You don't need to compare everything. It's worth it when there's no single right answer and coverage matters:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Reviewing a contract, a proposal or a plan&lt;/strong&gt; for gaps.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Technical design choices&lt;/strong&gt; and their trade-offs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Wording options&lt;/strong&gt; for an important email, pitch or headline.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Fact checks&lt;/strong&gt; — when one AI states something confidently, see whether the others agree.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. The manual way
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Copy your question, open each AI in its own tab, paste, send. For two assistants that's fine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For three or four it gets tedious, and the answers end up scattered across tabs, so reading them against each other takes effort too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;One rule that matters:&lt;/strong&gt; keep the question word-for-word identical. If the wording changes, you can't tell whether a difference comes from the model or from how you asked.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. A side-by-side model playground
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sites like LMArena have a side-by-side mode that sends one prompt to two models and shows both replies. It's a good way to get a feel for how models differ.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The trade-off: it's a separate place from your own ChatGPT, Claude or Gemini accounts, so those conversations don't land in your usual history, and you're not using the same settings and context you normally work with.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. Send once to your own AIs, then read the answers side by side
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/qarta-prompt-library-ai-c/pbnjcimookgjappnnlfknfoepfhgepng?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=seo-compare-en" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Qarta&lt;/a&gt;, a Chrome extension I build (full disclosure), can send one prompt to several AIs at once: pick the sites, send, and each one opens in its own tab with exactly the same text. Because it uses your normal accounts, every conversation also stays in that AI's own history.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F98xjwn2jidzdzxn00x37.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F98xjwn2jidzdzxn00x37.png" alt="Send one prompt to several AIs and read the answers side by side" width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the answers are in, Qarta's chat library lines them up on one screen, so you can see at a glance which reply is strongest. It works across ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Copilot (personal), Perplexity and Grok.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sending to several AIs at once and the side-by-side view are part of Pro ($10/month). On the free plan, every chat on all six AIs is still saved automatically, and the last 30 days are searchable in one place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Summary
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Two AIs, once in a while → do it by hand, with an identical question.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Exploring how models differ → a side-by-side playground.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Comparing your own AIs regularly → send once and read side by side with a tool like &lt;a href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/qarta-prompt-library-ai-c/pbnjcimookgjappnnlfknfoepfhgepng?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=seo-compare-en" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Qarta&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Feedback welcome — I read every message: &lt;a href="https://repech.com/qarta?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=seo-compare-en" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;repech.com/qarta&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


VedaX
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;VedaX turns Vedic Mathematics into a structured learning journey — helping learners learn techniques, practice with purpose, understand their mistakes, and build mastery.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Learn the technique. Practice with purpose. Master the skill.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;VedaX is a web application for learners who want to improve mental calculation speed using Vedic Mathematics. It combines:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a 16-technique Vedic Mathematics curriculum;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;technique explanations, examples, and guided practice;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a practice arena and timed challenge modes;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;daily challenges and streak tracking;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;progress, XP, level, mastery, mistakes, and recommendations;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;prerequisite-aware adaptive learning;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a deterministic mathematical engine that cross-validates every generated question;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a responsive React frontend and an authenticated Express/MongoDB backend.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The important design decision is that the browser is not trusted with learning outcomes. The server owns question generation, answer verification, attempts, scoring, XP, mastery, streaks, and reward idempotency.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Many mental-math learning apps are either static content libraries or client-only games. That creates two problems:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Learners do not get a clear path from a sutra explanation to measurable mastery.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A modified browser can change its own score, XP, streak, or answer state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;VedaX addresses both by pairing educational content with a verified mathematical engine and a server-authoritative learning model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The solution in one screen
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Choose a technique
↓
Learn the sutra and examples
↓
Receive a deterministic server-owned question
↓
Submit an answer
↓
Server verifies it against the stored question
↓
Attempt → score → XP → mastery → streak → recommendation
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



