&lt;/blockquote&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Technical Deliverability Checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before launching your email marketing campaigns, ensure your technical infrastructure is rock-solid:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;SPF (Sender Policy Framework):&lt;/strong&gt; Authorizes your sending server to send emails on behalf of your domain.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;DKIM (DomainKeys Identified Mail):&lt;/strong&gt; Adds a cryptographic digital signature to verify email authenticity.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;DMARC (Domain-based Message Authentication):&lt;/strong&gt; Protects your domain from spoofing and phishing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Warm-Up Period:&lt;/strong&gt; Ramp up sending volume gradually (10-20 emails/day per inbox) over 2 to 3 weeks.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Clean Data Source:&lt;/strong&gt; Ensure every contact list is freshly extracted through LeadsGen to keep bounce rates strictly under 2%.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Is cold B2B email marketing legal?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. In the United States, B2B cold emailing is regulated under the &lt;strong&gt;CAN-SPAM Act&lt;/strong&gt;, which permits cold outreach as long as you provide a clear opt-out mechanism, include your valid physical business address, and avoid deceptive subject lines. For European prospects, ensure compliance with GDPR by contacting legitimate business interests and respecting removal requests.&lt;/p&gt;

